<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<handout>
    <title>Methods for Solving Second-Order Differential Equations</title>

    <introduction>

        <p>
            In this activity you will work through five stations involving
            major concepts and solution methods for second-order differential
            equations.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each station:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li workspace="1 in">Show all work.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Explain your reasoning.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Compare your solution with your group members.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Be prepared to present your work to the class.</li>
        </ol>

    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="student-template">
        <title>Work Template</title>

        <p>
            Use the following structure for each problem.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li workspace="1 in">Identify the method that should be used.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Show all intermediate calculations.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Write the final answer clearly.</li>
            <li workspace="1 in">Verify your result whenever possible.</li>
        </ol>

    </subsection>

        
        <exercise>
        <title>Fundamental Sets of Solutions and the Wronskian</title>

        <p>
            Determine whether the given functions form a fundamental set of
            solutions for the differential equation.
        </p>
<ol><li workspace="1 in">
            <p>
                Consider
            </p>

            <me>
                y''-5y'+6y=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Determine whether
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=e^{2x},
                \qquad
                y_2=e^{3x}
            </me>

            <p>
                form a fundamental set of solutions.
            </p>

            <p>
                Compute the Wronskian.
            </p>
        </li><li workspace="1 in">
        
            <p>
                Consider
            </p>

            <me>
                y''-4y'+4y=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Determine whether
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=e^{2x},
                \qquad
                y_2=2e^{2x}
            </me>

            <p>
                form a fundamental set of solutions.
            </p>
            </li>   <li workspace="1 in">
            
            <p>
                Verify that
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=\cos x,
                \qquad
                y_2=\sin x
            </me>

            <p>
                are solutions of
            </p>

            <me>
                y''+y=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Compute the Wronskian and determine whether they form a
                fundamental set of solutions.
            </p>
            </li></ol>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
        <title>Undetermined Coefficients</title>
<ol><li workspace="2 in">
            <p>Solve using undetermined coefficients.</p>

            <me>
                y''-3y'+2y=4e^{3x}.
            </me></li><li workspace="2 in">

            <p>Solve using undetermined coefficients.</p>

            <me>
                y''+4y=8\cos(2x).
            </me>

            <p>
                Pay careful attention to resonance.
            </p>
            </li><li workspace="2 in">

            <p>Solve using undetermined coefficients.</p>

            <me>
                y''+y'-2y=(x+1)e^x.
            </me>
            </li></ol>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
        <title>Reduction of Order</title>
<ol><li workspace="2in">
            <p>
                One solution of
            </p>

            <me>
                y''-4y'+4y=0
            </me>

            <p>
                is
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=e^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Use reduction of order to find a second linearly independent
                solution.
            </p>
            </li><li workspace="2in">

            <p>
                One solution of
            </p>

            <me>
                x^2y''-3xy'+4y=0
            </me>

            <p>
                is
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=x^2.
            </me>

            <p>
                Use reduction of order to find a second solution.
            </p></li></ol>
        </exercise>
  
        <exercise>
          <title>Complementary and Particular Solutions</title>
<ol><li workspace="2in">
            <p>
                Find the complementary solution, a particular solution,
                and the general solution.
            </p>

            <me>
                y''+5y'+6y=12e^{-x}.
            </me>
            </li><li workspace="2in">

            <p>
                Find the complementary solution, a particular solution,
                and the general solution.
            </p>

            <me>
                y''+9y=18\sin(3x).
            </me></li></ol>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>

        <title>Variation of Parameters</title>
        <ol>
<li workspace="2in"><p>   Solve using variation of parameters.
            </p>
            <me>
                y''+y=\sec x.
            </me>
    </li><li workspace="2in">         <p>   Solve using variation of parameters.
            </p>

            <me>
                y''-y=\frac{1}{1+e^x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Keep your answer in integral form if necessary.
            </p>
            </li><li workspace="2in">
    <p>
        Solve the following differential equation using variation of
        parameters.
    </p>

    <me>
        x^2y''-3xy'+4y=x^3,
        \qquad x>0.
    </me>

    <p>
        Begin by finding the complementary solution. Then use variation of
        parameters to obtain a particular solution.
    </p>

    <p>
        Simplify your final answer as much as possible.
    </p>
        </li></ol>
        </exercise>

</handout>
